e live action required a much bigger  budget to pull off the impressive cgi we  see in the movie  instead of drawing out the scenes as  they did in the animated feature  favreau utilized techniques like green  screen and computer animation to realize  his film  this leads to another huge contrast  between each film and that's what we see  on screen  i'll go back to the pack i'll go back to  aquila  one change you might have noticed  between the two films is mowgli himself  in the animated version mowgli can be  described as happy-go-lucky and silly  he's not any different from any other 10  year old boy you might meet  and he was raised by wolves throughout  the film he consistently finds something  to laugh about even when he gets into  trouble  he's a naive boy or man cub as they say  in the film  and truly doesn't know any better that  aspect isn't lost on the 2016 version of  mowgli  but neil seti's acting and john  favreau's vision brings a newfound  seriousness to the character that the  1967 version lacks  mowgli has a deep connection to the  jungle and serious respect for nature  also unlike his animated counterpart he  is very much  aware of the dangers that the jungle  holds while the animated version  presents the jungle as a paradise  the live-action version reminds us that  the jungle is a scary  although beautiful place there's also  selflessness to his character that the  1967 mowgli  doesn't have someone's gotta do  something  when share khan becomes a threat mowgli  bravely volunteers to leave the wolf  pack and save them from facing the angry  tiger  in the animation mowgli complains the  entire time and whines to be brought  back to his home  he lacks the emotional depth that the  2016 version has  one thing they do have in common is that  neither one of them wants to leave the  jungle  however leaving the jungle seems to be  what's safest for mowgli in both  versions  and that's thanks to share khan the  villainous tiger plays a much bigger  role in the live action which is quite  the change from the original  he'll get mowgli while he's young and  helpless  in the animation we only hear about  share khan we don't actually see him  until over halfway through the film  all we know about him is what we're told  by other characters  we learned from bakira that share khan  dislikes humans and their technology  but that's about as far as his  discontentment goes the 2016 share khan  is hungry for revenge bearing a scar  from mowgli's father  something the animated version lacks  from the beginning of the film to the  very end  he proves to be a vengeful beast that  will not stop until he's accomplished  his goal of getting rid of mowgli once  and for all  the live action brings the drama and the  darkness and that's in part due to share  khan's terrifying nature and actions  opposite of share khan is bakira the  panther in both versions  bakhira is a watchful eye over mowgli  there's wisdom to both characters  but the audience can tell early on that  the 2016 version  is much more invested in mowgli serving  as his mentor  i know he's special i raised him we see  it in the very beginning when bakira  lectures mowgli about being less like a  man and more like a wolf  in the 1967 version bagheera states that  he checks in on the mancub  every now and again and sometimes walks  with him the 2016 bagheera has a deep  emotional connection to mowgli and the  audience can feel  it and of course who can forget  characters like baloo  and kah phil harris's rendition of the  character in 1967  is iconic who doesn't love to sing along  to the bare necessities  he's carefree and relaxed but he comes  to care for mowgli immediately  bill murray's baloo on the other hand is  more so lazy and uses mowgli to get  honey  but where harris's baloo wanted to teach  mowgli to be a bear  murray's baloo helps mowgli to embrace  himself as he is  instead of forcing him to be something  he isn't that's a message anyone can  take to heart  i say you could be a man right here  kha's role in the film undergoes a  change as well between the two films  we watch him try and fail to eat mowgli  twice in the animated feature  he's hypnotic with his colorful eyes and  slick voice but he's got  nothing on the 2016 version scarlett  johansson entrances mowgli  and the audience with her smooth melodic  voice but  she also has some information on  mowgli's backstory something we don't  get in the animated feature  even though kha is only seen once in the  2016 version  she leaves a lasting impact it's  terrifying to watch  kaw the snake almost consume mowgli but  in both versions he is saved by a  helpful hand  just a minute i've never seen one before  with all these changes in character it's  no wonder that the ending had to change  along with them  the 1967 version showed share khan face  mowgli after he had resolved to be alone  in the jungle  blue then comes into the fray to help  him then when lightning strikes and sets  fire to a nearby tree  mowgli turns to man's greatest weapon to  defeat sher khan  in the 2016 version it seems that mowgli  2 will use fire or the red flower  to best his opponent but we watch in awe  as he ultimately rejects it  instead he relies on his man cub  ingenuity to save him at the very end  then once the danger presented by share  khan vanishes the two mowgli's take  different paths in the 1967 version  following a young girl with a beautiful  singing voice  mowgli returns to human society however  in the 2016 version  after embracing his human nature mowgli  makes a choice to stay in the jungle  with his animal family with all of these  distinctions to consider  we have to ask which film is better we  took a look at the scores on rotten  tomatoes to try to answer the question  the 1967 version of the jungle book  received an 88  from critics time magazine remarked the  result is thoroughly delightful  the reasons for its success lie in  disney's own unfettered animal spirits 